The GPADC of the Allwinner A10, A13 and A20 holds the SoC's only
temperature sensor. Two problems with how the driver registers it as a
thermal zone:

Patch 1 is a fix for stable. In MFD mode the zone is registered with
devres on the parent device, so unloading the IIO module leaves it
registered with dangling ops and devdata; the thermal workqueue oopses
on the next poll. This was fixed once in 2017 and came back with the
conversion to the new OF thermal API in 2022. It applies on its own.

Patches 2 and 3 give the zone a hwmon interface, so the SoC temperature
reaches libsensors and the tools built on it (sensors, htop's CPU
temperature): today it is visible only in the thermal zone's sysfs.
devm_thermal_add_hwmon_sysfs() is declared in a header private to
drivers/thermal/, so patch 2 moves its declaration to <linux/thermal.h>;
patch 3 uses it the way sun8i_thermal does for the newer Allwinner SoCs.
The zone is "cpu-thermal" in the device trees, so the hwmon device is
"cpu_thermal", a name tools already treat as a CPU temperature. The
MFD's iio_hwmon cell already exposes the raw temperature on kernels with
CONFIG_SENSORS_IIO_HWMON (multi_v7_defconfig), as an anonymous
"iio_hwmon" chip; there it is now listed twice. Patch 3 says why the
named interface is still worth having.

Patch 2 touches the thermal core and patches 1 and 3 the IIO driver;
patch 3 depends on 2. If the thermal maintainers prefer, 2 and 3 can go
through one tree with an ack from the other.

Tested on an A10 board (Rikomagic MK802, kernel 6.18 with the series
backported): the unload/reload oops is gone over four cycles, and
cpu_thermal appears with the same reading as the zone. The A20 follows
the same MFD path; its test is pending.

D.R.racer (3):
  iio: adc: sun4i-gpadc: unregister the thermal zone with the IIO device
  thermal: hwmon: make devm_thermal_add_hwmon_sysfs() available outside
    the core
  iio: adc: sun4i-gpadc: add a hwmon interface for the thermal zone

